1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,120
And now for you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:08,020 --> 00:00:11,320
Hi, welcome to the Scoutmaster Podcast. This is Clark Green.

3
00:00:12,180 --> 00:00:16,820
Somebody once said, there's no such thing as a bad boy.

4
00:00:17,660 --> 00:00:25,880
And I agree, I do agree, but some of them can be a real pain every once in a while.

5
00:00:25,880 --> 00:00:29,420
Hey, this is podcast number two.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,400
Hey!

7
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:34,400
Hey!

8
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:36,400
Hey!

9
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:40,400
Hey!

10
00:00:40,400 --> 00:01:04,560
Scout Master Ship in seven minutes.

11
00:01:04,560 --> 00:01:14,580
Here are five ways to make the best of your opportunity as a scout leader.

12
00:01:14,720 --> 00:01:17,660
The first one is to trust the program.

13
00:01:17,820 --> 00:01:22,640
We have a program with 100 years of history and practice behind it, and it seems to work pretty well.

14
00:01:23,220 --> 00:01:28,760
Unfortunately, what we do sometimes is we try to reinvent it or we find it difficult to understand.

15
00:01:28,760 --> 00:01:35,420
But take everything in the program at face value and then put that to work for you.

16
00:01:36,340 --> 00:01:41,760
The program is not the same as it was when it was first rolled out in 1910.

17
00:01:42,440 --> 00:01:48,700
No, it's been changing and developing, and we have found better ways of doing things,

18
00:01:48,760 --> 00:01:52,760
and we have met the different cultural changes over the past 100 years.

19
00:01:52,760 --> 00:01:56,540
So when these changes come up, embrace the changes.

20
00:01:57,200 --> 00:01:58,020
Stick with them.

21
00:01:58,380 --> 00:01:59,060
Stay current.

22
00:01:59,980 --> 00:02:01,800
Don't shortcut things in the program.

23
00:02:01,980 --> 00:02:04,540
Don't try and make shortcuts or changes to the program.

24
00:02:04,820 --> 00:02:06,260
Don't try and make things harder.

25
00:02:06,460 --> 00:02:07,960
Don't try and make things easier.

26
00:02:08,420 --> 00:02:13,280
Because when you do these, what you're doing is you're diminishing the experience for your scouts.

27
00:02:13,980 --> 00:02:15,360
So trust the program.

28
00:02:16,240 --> 00:02:20,500
The next is to conduct activities that are age-appropriate.

29
00:02:20,500 --> 00:02:26,080
If you have a tiger cub den, you're conducting activities that are appropriate to boys in the first grade.

30
00:02:26,680 --> 00:02:35,900
If you have a venture crew, you are conducting activities that are appropriate to boys and girls from 14 to 21.

31
00:02:37,300 --> 00:02:41,980
You would not put a venture crew activity in a tiger cub den or vice versa.

32
00:02:42,740 --> 00:02:48,940
You wouldn't have a den of Weeblos do things that Boy Scouts are supposed to do,

33
00:02:48,940 --> 00:02:54,140
and you wouldn't have Boy Scouts do things that specifically a den of Weeblos are supposed to do.

34
00:02:54,520 --> 00:02:59,260
Sometimes we kind of jump the fence into the next pasture, and that doesn't work very well.

35
00:02:59,600 --> 00:03:03,280
So we need to concentrate on the age-appropriateness of our program.

36
00:03:03,900 --> 00:03:06,500
It takes an ego to be a scout leader.

37
00:03:07,180 --> 00:03:12,300
You've got to step, hey, and to do a podcast and a blog and, yeah.

38
00:03:12,300 --> 00:03:20,800
Yeah, but you've got to step up and be ready to take the reins and lead and be in front of people and things like that.

39
00:03:20,860 --> 00:03:21,880
So it takes a bit of an ego.

40
00:03:22,600 --> 00:03:31,080
So my next hint is to be ready to work with other adults, and that can be a bit of a problem for some of us

41
00:03:31,080 --> 00:03:34,620
because we're working with different family standards and expectations.

42
00:03:34,620 --> 00:03:42,880
We're working with people who might not see eye to eye with us as far as policy and the exact way that things should be done.

43
00:03:43,640 --> 00:03:46,840
So that makes it hard to accept help sometimes.

44
00:03:47,120 --> 00:03:50,100
We need to do those things, and we need to exercise a little professionalism.

45
00:03:50,180 --> 00:03:53,680
I'm not talking about being aloof and cold and distant.

46
00:03:53,820 --> 00:03:59,740
I'm talking about accepting the fact that we're all in this movement together and we all need to work together.

47
00:04:00,240 --> 00:04:03,720
Next is to maintain perspective and proportion.

48
00:04:03,720 --> 00:04:06,740
Don't let the perfect be the enemy of the good.

49
00:04:07,080 --> 00:04:11,200
All scouting, remember, is an individual standard based on a scout's effort.

50
00:04:11,780 --> 00:04:13,380
It's not the excellence of the program.

51
00:04:14,040 --> 00:04:18,600
It's the heart in it and the experience that your scouts are having.

52
00:04:19,279 --> 00:04:24,180
Keep your commitments to scouting and don't overextend them, and it's easy to do in scouting.

53
00:04:25,180 --> 00:04:29,640
I mean, we talk about the wearing of hats and the wearing of multiple hats,

54
00:04:30,640 --> 00:04:33,460
but keep the hat wearing to a minimum.

55
00:04:33,720 --> 00:04:38,280
You're not likely to encounter a new problem or difficulty.

56
00:04:38,880 --> 00:04:42,840
There's a world of solutions and resolutions and good ideas out there.

57
00:04:43,540 --> 00:04:51,680
For 100 years, we've been running, you know, basically a program that has had a pretty fixed set of goals and activities,

58
00:04:51,680 --> 00:04:54,600
and we've had the same problems.

59
00:04:54,740 --> 00:05:03,840
If you go back and you can find an old scouting magazine, go back as far as you can and look at what people were talking about,

60
00:05:03,900 --> 00:05:08,340
and they were talking about exactly the same things that we are dealing with today.

61
00:05:08,340 --> 00:05:14,560
You know, the same central issues around, well, we don't want to run a babysitting service,

62
00:05:14,940 --> 00:05:19,679
and the scouts don't seem to be very serious about some things, and blah, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah.

63
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:26,340
Yeah, that was the same thing we were talking about 100 years ago, and the solutions are very simple.

64
00:05:26,340 --> 00:05:33,140
It's not about your boy, if you're a parent, but it's all the scouts that you're working with, right?

65
00:05:33,659 --> 00:05:41,239
And that is something that can be difficult, because for an hour a week or whatever the length of the activity of your program is,

66
00:05:41,539 --> 00:05:44,380
you're a scout leader first, and you're a parent second.

67
00:05:44,380 --> 00:05:49,060
And that's going to bear a whole lot more discussion one of these days.

68
00:05:49,560 --> 00:05:55,800
Finally, focus on the goal, and the goal in scouting is always, always, always the success of your scouts.

69
00:05:56,340 --> 00:06:02,840
To prepare them for the next step in the program, to focus on the experience they're having rather than the process,

70
00:06:03,600 --> 00:06:08,180
and to strive to understand your role and maintain your scouting education.

71
00:06:09,020 --> 00:06:13,140
If you're a scoutmaster, you need to be a trained scoutmaster.

72
00:06:13,140 --> 00:06:21,659
If you're a scoutmaster with 10 years of tenure behind you, you need to think about going back and training again,

73
00:06:21,900 --> 00:06:25,760
availing yourself of every opportunity to learn more about your position.

74
00:06:26,479 --> 00:06:31,060
This goes for den leaders and cubmasters and venture crew advisors.

75
00:06:31,919 --> 00:06:37,520
We all need to keep current and to do our best to maintain a very good understanding of the role that we have in scouting.

76
00:06:37,520 --> 00:06:47,740
So those are five ways for you to take advantage and make the best opportunity of your time as a scout leader.

77
00:06:47,940 --> 00:06:48,740
Trust the program.

78
00:06:49,640 --> 00:06:51,740
Conduct activities that are age appropriate.

79
00:06:52,359 --> 00:06:54,000
Be ready to work with other adults.

80
00:06:54,859 --> 00:06:57,140
Try to maintain perspective and proportion.

81
00:06:57,799 --> 00:06:58,820
And focus on the goal.

82
00:06:58,820 --> 00:07:27,600
This has to be the truth, folks, because there is no way.

83
00:07:27,600 --> 00:07:31,020
Anyway, anyone could make this up.

84
00:07:34,020 --> 00:07:40,200
Herman was an older fella who, I think his wife kicked him out of the house every summer,

85
00:07:41,140 --> 00:07:43,220
and told him to go find something to do.

86
00:07:45,840 --> 00:07:48,420
He used to show up at our scout camp.

87
00:07:49,040 --> 00:07:51,740
He had been a scouter, and he was retired,

88
00:07:52,340 --> 00:07:54,980
and so we would make Herman the chaplain.

89
00:07:55,680 --> 00:07:58,360
And he served in this capacity for a number of years,

90
00:07:58,720 --> 00:08:01,720
and he was a pretty good chaplain at times.

91
00:08:02,180 --> 00:08:04,740
And he was quite a character.

92
00:08:05,460 --> 00:08:08,420
And I remember him very well because one morning he showed up

93
00:08:08,420 --> 00:08:11,640
at the Nature Lodge with a pillowcase.

94
00:08:18,420 --> 00:08:26,060
Now, you know, we in our part of the world have lots of interesting reptiles and amphibians,

95
00:08:26,560 --> 00:08:31,440
and probably the one that causes the greatest interest is the copperhead snake

96
00:08:31,440 --> 00:08:33,180
because it is a poisonous snake.

97
00:08:33,820 --> 00:08:39,360
Not deadly poisonous, but certainly an unpleasant thing to run into and to be bitten by.

98
00:08:40,440 --> 00:08:47,680
So anyway, Herman hands the pillowcase over to the fellas in the Nature Lodge

99
00:08:47,680 --> 00:08:49,900
and proclaims that he has brought them a snake.

100
00:08:50,760 --> 00:08:52,880
And they said, well, Herman, where did you find it?

101
00:08:52,880 --> 00:08:55,239
He says, well, it was out on the rocks this morning, sunning itself.

102
00:08:55,339 --> 00:08:57,900
Well, I was out walking around this morning,

103
00:08:58,199 --> 00:09:02,579
and so I went back to my cabin and grabbed my pillowcase and got a stick

104
00:09:02,579 --> 00:09:05,939
and grabbed the snake and brought it over to you.

105
00:09:06,020 --> 00:09:07,439
And they said, well, Herman, what kind is it?

106
00:09:07,439 --> 00:09:08,880
He says, well, I was hoping you would tell me.

107
00:09:08,880 --> 00:09:17,640
Can you guess what happens next?

108
00:09:20,620 --> 00:09:22,040
Yep, you're right.

109
00:09:22,540 --> 00:09:26,760
It was a copperhead, and I think Herman almost had a heart attack right there.

110
00:09:26,760 --> 00:09:32,220
But somehow he managed to survive and went on to be our chaplain for a number of years.

111
00:09:32,220 --> 00:10:02,200
Thank you.

112
00:10:02,220 --> 00:10:09,440
So this is installment number two in our series about instructional methods for scouts.

113
00:10:10,180 --> 00:10:13,380
What we're going to discuss here is guided discovery.

114
00:10:13,760 --> 00:10:14,760
Guided discovery.

115
00:10:15,640 --> 00:10:19,280
So here's a good practical application of it.

116
00:10:19,840 --> 00:10:25,780
I'm going to instruct guys in some totenship skills about the safe handling of a knife

117
00:10:25,780 --> 00:10:28,440
and how to maintain it, keep it clean.

118
00:10:28,440 --> 00:10:35,560
So I'm going to have all the stuff I need to, you know, maintain a knife and safely use it

119
00:10:35,560 --> 00:10:38,040
laid out on a table in front of me.

120
00:10:38,500 --> 00:10:42,260
And the scouts are going to come and stand in front of that table,

121
00:10:42,400 --> 00:10:44,700
and I'm going to start asking them questions.

122
00:10:44,920 --> 00:10:46,920
I'm going to say, hey, what have I got on the table here?

123
00:10:47,320 --> 00:10:51,180
Well, you've got a knife and a sharpening stone or whatever that is,

124
00:10:51,180 --> 00:10:56,180
and a rag and a little oil and, you know, a piece of wood.

125
00:10:56,900 --> 00:11:01,099
And I said, now, what do you think we're going to do here?

126
00:11:01,300 --> 00:11:03,439
Well, we're going to learn how to use knives, right?

127
00:11:03,680 --> 00:11:10,500
So, well, where can you learn the skills about and rules about handling a knife safely?

128
00:11:10,959 --> 00:11:12,199
Well, you're going to tell us, right?

129
00:11:12,280 --> 00:11:12,420
Yeah.

130
00:11:12,420 --> 00:11:14,219
But is there any other place that you can learn?

131
00:11:14,979 --> 00:11:18,060
Well, yeah, it's in the Scout handbook.

132
00:11:18,219 --> 00:11:19,579
Oh, what part of the Scout handbook?

133
00:11:20,699 --> 00:11:21,860
Oh, geez, I don't know.

134
00:11:21,920 --> 00:11:22,599
I saw it once.

135
00:11:22,839 --> 00:11:27,020
And, you know, a couple of boys would start madly flipping through the handbook hoping to find it.

136
00:11:27,339 --> 00:11:29,740
They'd say, look, does the book have an index?

137
00:11:30,160 --> 00:11:30,800
Yeah, it does.

138
00:11:31,300 --> 00:11:31,979
Oh, okay.

139
00:11:31,979 --> 00:11:37,380
Can you find the area that deals with how to use a pocket knife?

140
00:11:37,860 --> 00:11:39,000
And somebody will find that.

141
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:46,340
And I said, so can you read the first paragraph about sharpening a pocket knife out loud?

142
00:11:47,180 --> 00:11:54,500
So, yeah, now they're reading the paragraph and, you know, things progress on from there.

143
00:11:54,820 --> 00:11:57,080
So do you understand what guided discovery is now?

144
00:11:57,480 --> 00:11:58,480
It's pretty simple.

145
00:11:58,480 --> 00:12:00,840
It's a set of questions, okay?

146
00:12:00,840 --> 00:12:12,860
I mean, what we're doing is we're embracing the principle that Scouts learn best, that we all learn best, when they are more in control of the process.

147
00:12:13,520 --> 00:12:16,180
And we respect their natural inquisitiveness.

148
00:12:16,580 --> 00:12:21,560
And we inspire it and we challenge it to work things out for themselves.

149
00:12:21,560 --> 00:12:39,680
Now, let's imagine that the guys came and stood around the table and I started to give them a lecture about the safety of knives and the rules and things like that, along with a handout, okay?

150
00:12:39,680 --> 00:12:50,199
I would have about, what do you figure, folks, about three to five minutes before I had totally lost them and they're staring out into space.

151
00:12:50,739 --> 00:12:56,140
And their hand is wandering, you know, over the table for the pocket knife.

152
00:12:56,680 --> 00:13:01,680
And, you know, I have to look at them and say, hey, fellas, now you need to pay attention, okay?

153
00:13:01,680 --> 00:13:10,479
So do you see the difference between the lecture type of instruction and the guided discovery type?

154
00:13:10,760 --> 00:13:13,660
And which do you think is going to be more effective with the guys?

155
00:13:13,939 --> 00:13:16,140
And am I using guided discovery right now?

156
00:13:18,900 --> 00:13:20,260
It becomes a habit.

157
00:13:20,260 --> 00:13:34,000
Now, I have in the PDF file that I referred to in our first post on this, which is available at scoutmaster.typepad.com.

158
00:13:34,460 --> 00:13:37,240
Again, go to the right-hand column.

159
00:13:38,180 --> 00:13:45,740
Use the Google search box on the blog for instructional and you'll be able to find these posts and the PDF.

160
00:13:45,740 --> 00:14:00,060
But, you know, there's a list, a couple of lists of different types of questions that help you use this guided discovery method.

161
00:14:00,980 --> 00:14:02,940
You know, what would be an example of that?

162
00:14:03,060 --> 00:14:03,800
How do you know?

163
00:14:04,240 --> 00:14:05,360
Why do you think that's true?

164
00:14:05,440 --> 00:14:07,000
Do you have any evidence that it's true?

165
00:14:07,260 --> 00:14:08,700
What difference does that make?

166
00:14:09,140 --> 00:14:10,860
What are your reasons for saying that?

167
00:14:11,040 --> 00:14:13,440
What other information do you need?

168
00:14:13,440 --> 00:14:17,800
If you're a student of philosophy, you know that what we're talking about is the Socratic method.

169
00:14:18,320 --> 00:14:22,000
In other words, we describe a thing by asking questions about it.

170
00:14:22,340 --> 00:14:25,940
It's pretty simple, but it's not a gimmick.

171
00:14:26,900 --> 00:14:38,220
It's a way for you to respect and encourage the natural curiosity that scouts have and allow them to discover some things for themselves.

172
00:14:38,220 --> 00:14:49,360
Now, the other thing I will tell you is that the little reading trick there where we found the section of the Scout Handbook and I asked for one Scout to read the first paragraph out loud.

173
00:14:49,660 --> 00:14:53,200
Well, then it would go to the next Scout and he would read the next paragraph out loud.

174
00:14:53,580 --> 00:14:55,180
I could just lecture that.

175
00:14:55,300 --> 00:14:56,720
I could just read it out loud myself.

176
00:14:57,200 --> 00:14:58,220
I'm perfectly capable.

177
00:14:58,220 --> 00:15:03,220
But why not have them involved in it, reading it?

178
00:15:03,420 --> 00:15:04,640
They're going to be on their toes.

179
00:15:04,820 --> 00:15:06,480
They're going to be considering what it is.

180
00:15:06,560 --> 00:15:12,000
And you're not going to be able to insert a lot of extraneous information that nobody really needs.

181
00:15:13,200 --> 00:15:17,000
So it's going to be sweet and smart and to the point.

182
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:23,600
So we're going to pick up on this again in our next podcast, talking about instructional methods for Scouts.

183
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:59,180
Cliff Young was 62 years old in 1984.

184
00:16:00,540 --> 00:16:03,440
62 is no spring chicken, you would imagine.

185
00:16:04,460 --> 00:16:06,580
And Cliff lived in Australia.

186
00:16:07,960 --> 00:16:13,600
He got interested in one of the world's most grueling ultramarathons.

187
00:16:13,600 --> 00:16:23,940
This is Australia's 543 mile endurance race that takes its participants from Sydney to Melbourne.

188
00:16:25,260 --> 00:16:27,500
And it usually lasts about five days.

189
00:16:27,640 --> 00:16:33,440
Winter usually takes about five days to complete just an incredibly punishing course.

190
00:16:34,120 --> 00:16:42,000
And this is one of those runs that's attempted by intensely trained world-class athletes

191
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:46,660
that are, you know, some of the oldest ones are half of Cliff's age.

192
00:16:48,280 --> 00:16:54,780
Cliff placed 7th in 1984 at the age of 62.

193
00:16:55,360 --> 00:16:59,560
He placed 7th in that race.

194
00:17:01,300 --> 00:17:05,859
Mostly because he had a displaced hip that he got during the race.

195
00:17:07,020 --> 00:17:08,619
That was in 1984.

196
00:17:08,619 --> 00:17:15,679
In 1983, when he was 61 years old, Cliff Young took first place.

197
00:17:18,099 --> 00:17:19,280
Yeah, believe it or not.

198
00:17:19,500 --> 00:17:21,579
He showed up in overalls and work boots.

199
00:17:22,199 --> 00:17:25,020
And he looked pretty much like a spectator.

200
00:17:25,839 --> 00:17:31,979
But then he went and registered and picked up a race number and headed for the starting line.

201
00:17:31,980 --> 00:17:37,380
And some of the other curious athletes said, are you serious?

202
00:17:37,460 --> 00:17:38,720
You're going to run this race?

203
00:17:39,780 --> 00:17:43,380
And they said, you know, seriously?

204
00:17:44,980 --> 00:17:51,800
No offense, but you can't even finish this race, much less, you know, try and run it.

205
00:17:53,020 --> 00:17:54,240
But Cliff knew better.

206
00:17:54,240 --> 00:17:57,200
He was raised on a farm.

207
00:17:57,920 --> 00:18:01,860
And it was a farm too poor to afford horses or tractors.

208
00:18:03,140 --> 00:18:05,300
This is in the outback of Australia.

209
00:18:06,740 --> 00:18:16,880
So growing up, it was his responsibility whenever storms would roll in to round up about 2,000 sheep spread across about the same number of acres.

210
00:18:16,880 --> 00:18:20,280
2,000 sheep spread across 2,000 acres.

211
00:18:21,440 --> 00:18:26,560
And sometimes Cliff had to run for two or three days straight just to round up all the sheep.

212
00:18:28,480 --> 00:18:32,480
Cliff's secret was that he really didn't run, not like we think of running.

213
00:18:32,880 --> 00:18:34,900
He had this kind of weird shuffling gait.

214
00:18:34,900 --> 00:18:41,440
And when the race started, Cliff got left behind.

215
00:18:42,820 --> 00:18:52,940
As the more experienced athletes took off, he just kind of shuffled off the finish line or the starting line there and started moving.

216
00:18:53,380 --> 00:18:55,160
And he was left behind.

217
00:18:55,160 --> 00:19:02,920
Now, most of the participants in this Sydney to Melbourne race ran 18 hours a day.

218
00:19:03,300 --> 00:19:05,820
And they slept for six hours a day.

219
00:19:07,140 --> 00:19:14,240
And on the morning of the second day, Cliff was still in the race.

220
00:19:15,120 --> 00:19:17,980
But not only that, he had been jogging all night.

221
00:19:19,140 --> 00:19:21,840
And somebody asked him, and they said, well, when are you going to sleep?

222
00:19:21,900 --> 00:19:23,320
And he said, well, I'm not going to sleep.

223
00:19:23,440 --> 00:19:24,540
I'm going to run straight through.

224
00:19:25,160 --> 00:19:29,600
543 miles.

225
00:19:31,600 --> 00:19:35,540
By the final night, Cliff had passed all the young world-class athletes.

226
00:19:35,960 --> 00:19:38,480
And he was the first competitor to cross the finish line.

227
00:19:38,820 --> 00:19:40,740
And he even set a new course record.

228
00:19:41,880 --> 00:19:49,100
He was genuinely surprised when somebody approached him to give him $10,000 for his first place finish.

229
00:19:49,780 --> 00:19:51,400
He didn't even know about the prize.

230
00:19:51,400 --> 00:19:58,060
What he did is he took his $10,000 and gave his winnings to several other runners.

231
00:19:58,060 --> 00:20:06,120
And this was something that endured him to all of Australia and made him a true hero.

232
00:20:06,120 --> 00:20:10,719
So Cliff went on to run that race several times.

233
00:20:10,719 --> 00:20:23,860
And in 1997, at the age of 76, Cliff was attempting to raise money for homeless children by running around the border of Australia.

234
00:20:23,860 --> 00:20:24,459
He didn't finish.

235
00:20:24,459 --> 00:20:26,699
He didn't finish.

236
00:20:27,340 --> 00:20:27,600
Nope.

237
00:20:27,820 --> 00:20:40,300
He only completed 4,051 miles before he was pretty much forced to stop because his single crew member became ill.

238
00:20:40,300 --> 00:20:51,500
This guy shuffled in his little shuffling run for 4,051 miles and probably would have finished.

239
00:20:52,060 --> 00:20:54,540
But his single crew member fell ill.

240
00:20:56,060 --> 00:21:01,379
Cliff Young passed away in 2003 at the age of 81.

241
00:21:01,380 --> 00:21:12,140
At least three champions of the Sydney to Melbourne Marathon have won using the energy-saving Young Shuffle.

242
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:16,560
Competitors no longer sleep during the race.

243
00:21:17,500 --> 00:21:22,100
Just like Cliff, that most unlikely champion.

244
00:21:23,180 --> 00:21:28,560
One can imagine him resenting the hard work that he did when he was rounding up sheep when he was a boy.

245
00:21:28,560 --> 00:21:31,080
But instead, he just kind of lived with it.

246
00:21:31,500 --> 00:21:33,000
And he applied what he learned.

247
00:21:33,179 --> 00:21:35,100
And he changed the way people do things.

248
00:21:37,000 --> 00:21:45,200
I don't know that any of us will ever imitate the scale and just jaw-dropping awesomeness of Cliff's accomplishments.

249
00:21:47,139 --> 00:21:54,560
But some unlikely experience will have prepared us to achieve in some unlikely manner.

250
00:21:58,560 --> 00:22:22,840
Well, thanks for listening to the second edition of the Scoutmasters podcast.

251
00:22:22,840 --> 00:22:33,340
I always appreciate when people listen and when people read the blog at scoutmaster.typepad.com.

252
00:22:33,899 --> 00:22:37,639
And I always appreciate your comments, your feedback, your questions.

